Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Product needs to define successful channel adoption as sustained useful activ=
ity rather than enrolment or one event. Failure anecdotes are beginning to dr=
ive decisions, although retries and late posting may be making the problem lo=
ok larger than the affected-customer population. There are enough data except=
ions to make a polished average dangerous; preserve the unresolved cases.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if adoption differs by product and customer ne=
ed?
2. What evidence would instead support that first successful use predicts con=
tinued use?
3. How do we rule out that customers return to assisted channels after failur=
e?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use December 2021 as the new evidence point rath=
er than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards which onboardin=
g journey and success metric should change. A useful output would be a one-pa=
ge finding note plus the underlying CSV for our own review.

Use monthly account snapshots, status events, limits, product enrolments and =
signatories; transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, statuses and er=
ror context; customer contacts, complaints and suggestions; ATM attempts, hos=
t responses, PIN attempts, blocked-card state and latency. One caution: Avail=
able data supports channel behaviour but not every app screen, click or aband=
oned session.

Regards
